<article class="env-lemma" id="09JX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/09JX">Lemma <span data-tag="09JX">22.7.5</span>.</a> Let $(A, \text{d})$ be a differential graded algebra. Let $L_1 \to L_2 \to \ldots \to L_ n$ be a sequence of composable homomorphisms of differential graded $A$-modules. There exists a commutative diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ L_1 \ar[r] &  L_2 \ar[r] &  \ldots \ar[r] &  L_ n \\  M_1 \ar[r] \ar[u] &  M_2 \ar[r] \ar[u] &  \ldots \ar[r] &  M_ n \ar[u] }  \]
</div>
  <p> in $\text{Mod}_{(A, \text{d})}$ such that each $M_ i \to M_{i + 1}$ is an admissible monomorphism and each $M_ i \to L_ i$ is a homotopy equivalence. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The case $n = 1$ is without content. Lemma <a href="/tag/09JW" data-tag="09JW">22.7.4</a> is the case $n = 2$. Suppose we have constructed the diagram except for $M_ n$. Apply Lemma <a href="/tag/09JW" data-tag="09JW">22.7.4</a> to the composition $M_{n - 1} \to L_{n - 1} \to L_ n$. The result is a factorization $M_{n - 1} \to M_ n \to L_ n$ as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>